WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.174 --> 00:00:17.510
- Hello, everyone, welcome
to the Khan Academy

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.311
Daily Homeroom livestream.

00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:22.560
For those of y'all who are new to this.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.250
This is our way of staying connected

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.480
in times of school closures.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.520
Obviously Khan Academy is a not for profit

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.790
with a mission of providing
a free world class education

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.410
for anyone, anywhere.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.870
Over the last many years,
we've been building resources

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.840
in math, the sciences,
English and language arts,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.110
the social sciences, to
help you feel supported,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:40.950
whether you're a student

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.320
who's learning outside of the classroom,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.070
or whether you're a teacher
trying to support students

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:46.830
in the classroom, differentiating
instruction, et cetera.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.440
But when we saw the
school closures happen,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.585
we realized that we have a
fairly unique role to play,

00:00:50.585 --> 00:00:52.911
to help keep people learning to support

00:00:52.911 --> 00:00:56.470
all of you as parents, as
teachers and as students.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:57.380
And as part of that,

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.210
beyond just putting the resources out

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:00.180
that we've already had.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.720
And the calendars, the
schedules, the learning plans,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.770
we've been running parent
and teacher webinars.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:05.830
We also wanted to do this

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.640
as just a way to answer questions,

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.020
feel connected and bring on
some really interesting guests

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.880
who can give us more perspective on

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.870
how do we keep learning
and the world as a whole.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:14.980
And we have a really great guest

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.700
I'm going to introduce in a little bit.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.140
I do like to remind everyone,

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.890
Khan Academy is a not for profit,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.634
we only can exist through
philanthropic donations

00:01:22.634 --> 00:01:24.810
from folks like yourself,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.040
I want to give a special shout
out to several corporations

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.700
that have stepped up in the last few weeks

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.060
to to help our COVID response.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.120
We were already running
at a deficit before this.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.550
And now we're seeing our
traffic at about two to three ex

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.500
of what it typically is

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.300
and we're trying to actually accelerate

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.280
a lot of programs and do new things.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.097
So special thanks to Bank of
America, AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:01:45.097 --> 00:01:48.610
Fastly and Novartis
for making that happen.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.140
With that, I'm excited
to introduce our guest,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.900
Laura Overdeck.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.260
Laura, there's a lot of different,

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.044
many interesting things to
talk about with Laura, today.

00:02:00.044 --> 00:02:03.460
One of is, you know I have to thank Laura

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.120
she's a longtime
supporter of Khan Academy.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.700
She makes a lot of what
we're able to do possible

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.400
hopefully all of you are able to enjoy.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.970
And Laura, I don't know if I'm hearing,

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.310
I'm hearing like my own voice through...

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.837
Oh, it's fine I'll just power through it.

00:02:18.837 --> 00:02:22.047
- I'm hearing an echo too, but...

00:02:22.047 --> 00:02:26.163
- Okay, anything we can
do to, on the echo side.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.730
- You know what, let me,
I'm gonna try one thing.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.040
- That got rid of the echo.

00:02:34.945 --> 00:02:35.967
- Is that a little better?

00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:37.910
- That's a lot better.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.230
It's still going on a little
bit in the background,

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.280
but we'll power through this.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.010
Anyway, as I was introducing Laura.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.250
Laura has been a longtime
supporter of Khan Academy,

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:46.911
through the Overdeck Family Foundation

00:02:46.911 --> 00:02:49.600
we definitely sent her positive karma

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.520
for making all of this possible.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.010
But also to this conversation.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.797
Laura is the founder of
Bedtime Math and Fun Factor.

00:02:57.797 --> 00:03:00.760
And Bedtime Math and Fun
Factor are very relevant

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.050
even before COVID crisis.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.090
But I think in this
crisis when a lot of kids

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.610
are learning from home and parents

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.430
are trying to figure out
what are enriching activities

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.210
to do with them that
are that more relevant.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.640
So Laura, thanks for joining us.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.780
I hope you and your
family are doing all right

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.486
in these crazy times.

00:03:16.486 --> 00:03:19.790
Tell us all a little
bit about Bedtime Math

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.083
and now this new project, Fun Factor.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.600
- Sure, so we...

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.250
Bedtime Math I started
about eight years ago,

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.020
it really is truly one of those things

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.940
that started in the garage,

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.300
except it was in the bedroom,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.160
although not that kind of
bedroom, a kid's bedroom.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.380
But basically, my husband and I

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.430
would read our kids a bedtime story

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.120
and then almost without thinking about it,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.050
we'd give them a fun math problem

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.060
just something we'd make up on the fly.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.900
And when we started sharing these

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.300
with other people, it grew really fast.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.930
And I think that's because people

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.850
were very hungry for math to be fun.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.900
And also to be real life for kids.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.870
You know, a lot of times we take math

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.670
and try to make it fun.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.400
At Bedtime Math, we find the thing

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:08.950
that's already fun for kids,

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.460
and then find the math in it

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.060
and that's a very different
way of thinking about it.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:15.400
Once we started that,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.190
we have an app, we have a website,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.490
the math problem goes out every day.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.630
We also started an after school math club

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.690
called Crazy Eights, with hands on games.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.470
And what's happened with this crisis

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.725
is we're actually now adapting those games

00:04:28.725 --> 00:04:32.720
for teachers to use with
their students from afar.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:33.970
And that's what Fun Factor is.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:35.270
That's our new initiative.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:37.560
- And make that a little more tangible.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.700
What age groups is this appropriate for?

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.610
I guess, you could say skill
levels and what is it like?

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.180
Are these the standard math problems

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.220
that you might see as
part of your curriculum?

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.820
Are they that plus other things?

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.580
- Yeah, so it really started off because

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.340
it was parallel with
reading a bedtime story.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.580
It's really for those ages

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:58.860
when you would read a book to kids.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.014
So we say ages two to nine

00:05:01.014 --> 00:05:03.810
two sounds aggressive, but it's not.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.670
It's just that's our mentality

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.560
that people are like, "Ooh,
math is tedious and dry."

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:10.370
It's not math is wonderful.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.100
And you can totally start
with a two year old,

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.880
which is what we did with our kids.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.210
And the math problems are
pretty different in spirit

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.790
from what you would find in a textbook,

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.770
because of course, we can
talk about whatever we want.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.100
We write them about pillow
forts, ninjas, drafts,

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.550
chocolate chips, just
stuff that kids love.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:33.110
And then there will be three
or four levels of questions

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:35.100
about what we just talked about.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.790
And those range from like
a Pre-K level question.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:41.050
That might be, you
know, giraffes or brown,

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.990
find three brown things in the room,

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.570
all the way up to third graders

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.083
doing multiplication and logic problems.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.890
- I have to give a testimonial

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.471
I've been using you obviously

00:05:51.471 --> 00:05:53.690
through you I learned about Bedtime Math

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:55.310
near the beginning, you know,

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:57.924
many many years ago and
my oldest who's now 11.

00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:01.300
I was using it with him
when he was five, six, seven

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.790
years old and he definitely enjoyed it.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:05.310
And I, you know, then
used it with my daughter

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.410
and I'm using it now with my youngest,

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:11.640
so a personal testimonial for that.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.210
You know, taking it one step above that,

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.900
you know, you are, given
all of your focus and energy

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.530
and education generally
and on Bedtime Math

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.157
and Crazy Eights and Fun Factor.

00:06:21.157 --> 00:06:23.100
You know, I think you have a good lens

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.110
on how do parents deal with
this type of situation,

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:28.260
especially with younger kids.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.440
A lot of parents are worried

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.638
that maybe my kids aren't learning enough.

00:06:31.638 --> 00:06:32.870
What advice do you have?

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:34.900
I know you have, your kids
are a little bit older now.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:37.820
But what advice do you have

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.120
for parents trying to
deal with this scenario?

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:46.990
- So I would say this crisis has really,

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:49.350
I think shone a bright
light on a lot of things

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:50.400
with education and we should

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.400
absolutely take advantage of it.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.970
So one thing is that with kids now home

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.140
and liberated from their classroom,

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:01.830
you can see your child,
where they're stuck

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.520
and where they're really flying.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.240
And that's something that when kids

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.250
are all together with their
classmates in our school system,

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:09.630
which mostly, unfortunately,

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.890
is fairly lockstep everybody does

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.340
chapter three on September 28th,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.950
everybody does chapter four,

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:17.980
on October 2nd.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:18.813
Kids have gaps.

00:07:18.813 --> 00:07:20.490
And I know you've talked
about this so much

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.310
the Swiss cheese learning where kids

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.534
have their own particular
holes that didn't get filled.

00:07:25.534 --> 00:07:28.800
Now that everybody's home,
and they have more time,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.510
'cause you're not in transit
on a bus, between classes.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:34.780
There's a real opportunity for parents

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:38.960
and kids to figure out where
they could use more practice.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:40.800
And there are so many resources out there

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.270
and of course, Khan
Academy, I mean, likewise,

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.710
my kids of all use Khan Academy,
we use it to learn things,

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.830
you can fill in any topic that
you wanna learn more about.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.126
And I just think that when I
really look at what's going on

00:07:53.126 --> 00:07:57.829
in our education system, we've
really pushed kids through

00:07:57.829 --> 00:08:00.360
when they are not ready
for the next topic,

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:01.890
when they haven't mastered

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.080
and where I've seen this most acutely is,

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.560
I actually tutor in our
women's state prison

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.060
here in New Jersey.

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.720
And the insiders, it's women
who have a high school degree

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.970
and they wanna get their associates.

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:16.720
And I'm working on pre algebra with them.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.060
I mean, someone's given
them a high school diploma,

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:24.450
signifying that they have
this mastery, and they don't.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.193
And it's not right that we do that.

00:08:26.193 --> 00:08:28.088
It's because they've been moved along

00:08:28.088 --> 00:08:29.824
without anybody filling those gaps.

00:08:29.824 --> 00:08:32.930
What's interesting is these
women are so motivated

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:35.310
because of course, they've
now got a double whammy.

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:37.120
They know it's going to
be hard to get a job,

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:37.953
when they get out.

00:08:37.953 --> 00:08:39.990
They've got another strike against them.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:42.830
But when I sit with them, they do learn

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.750
so they didn't fall through the cracks

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:45.950
'cause they couldn't learn out.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:47.050
They fell through the cracks

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:48.550
'cause there's something in our system

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.340
not filling those gaps.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.130
And I'm hoping that with this crisis,

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:53.993
we can really tackle that.

00:08:55.021 --> 00:08:55.854
- No that's incredible.

00:08:55.854 --> 00:08:58.250
And Laura, I'm getting a message

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:00.830
from the tech team, you
might have to tabs open

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:02.090
that are on Facebook or YouTube.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.490
And so they're getting the live stream.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.700
So they're saying if you
could close all of your tabs

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:07.120
that are not this one,

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.320
that might be where the
echo is coming from.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.360
And I'll give a message
while Laura is figuring out

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.210
if that is the case.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.340
For any of y'all with questions.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.920
We're seeing questions come on YouTube,

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:20.990
ask Laura and I anything

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:23.210
and we have team members
who are looking at them.

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.340
And we're going to surface them.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.520
And actually, I'll start
with a question from YouTube.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:28.353
And that did help.

00:09:28.353 --> 00:09:29.440
Thanks, Laura.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.130
So this is from YouTube.
Nekeil Govender asks,

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:34.540
asks us both, but I'm more
curious about your answer.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:36.317
I'm happy to give a little bit as well.

00:09:36.317 --> 00:09:38.197
"What was your inspiration to help others

00:09:38.197 --> 00:09:39.680
"in terms of education?"

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.620
And I guess you know,
why focus on education

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.213
of all the things in the world?

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:47.900
- Well, again, this was
not terribly premeditated.

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:49.330
When we started Bedtime Math.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:51.990
We were just kinda doing
this for fun with our kids.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:53.870
But when we shared the math problems,

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:55.790
what happened first is basically

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:59.370
there are a lot of hyper
competitive parents in my town

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.510
and they wanted to know what
we were doing with our kids.

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:04.050
So I started sharing the
math problems to people.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:09.050
And I was stunned at how these
college educated parents,

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.310
many of them Ivy League, were so nervous

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:15.320
that I hadn't put the
answers to the questions.

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.077
And they'd say to me, "I
know five plus two is seven,

00:10:18.077 --> 00:10:20.720
"but I feel better if you put
the answer at the bottom,"

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:23.120
because initially, I
didn't put the answers.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.530
And, you know, it just
really was a wake up call

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:28.660
about what math anxiety is out there.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:30.550
You know, we have a country of people

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:33.340
who are nervous about calculating the tip.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:36.530
And this includes high
school grads, college grads,

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:37.850
they're nervous about the tip,

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:40.440
and that is a fourth and
fifth grade math skill,

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:41.563
as you well know.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.800
It's not that hard, and I truly
believe everybody can do it.

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:46.880
And we have to get there

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:50.530
because I think when you talk
about the fall of an empire,

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:52.700
I mean, we're doing a lot of
things wrong in this country,

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.170
but having people afraid
of fourth grade math

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:56.830
is not helping.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:11:00.230
So it just really lit a fire
under me to get out there

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:03.411
and see if I could move
the needle on this.

00:11:03.411 --> 00:11:04.244
- Now, that makes me
think about two things.

00:11:04.244 --> 00:11:07.350
One is there is just this
so many people's brains

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.670
just something happened to them, trauma,

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:11.700
something where math they just shut down.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.550
I mean, your example of,
you know, Ivy League grads,

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.930
wanting the comfort of
an answer to a, you know,

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:19.958
first grade, or kindergarten type problem.

00:11:19.958 --> 00:11:22.750
And just for any of y'all who
think that tip calculation

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:26.300
is just valuable for
calculating tips, which it is,

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.670
you know, this is actually
an interview question.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:28.870
We used to use a Khan Academy

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:30.870
for a lot of entry level
jobs where we just said,

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:32.060
we just give a little word problem

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:33.690
you in five friends go to a restaurant,

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.380
you all order this, this, this,

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.100
you wanna pay the right amount of tip,

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:38.980
someone pays this amount,
how much should you pay?

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.440
Or how much change should there be?

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.606
And you'd be surprised how many people

00:11:42.606 --> 00:11:46.453
with college degrees had trouble
answering those questions.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:49.590
So you might see this in
an interview question.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:51.240
And I'll just add to Laura, you know,

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:52.450
if you think about education,

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:53.810
it's just a single leverage point

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:55.560
that I think builds on
everything else, you know,

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.250
human capital is the ultimate capital

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:01.300
and if we can let that grow
it'll go to positive uses.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.440
If you don't, not only will it atrophy,

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.100
but it could go in a negative direction.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:06.862
So that's my two cents.

00:12:06.862 --> 00:12:11.357
So from YouTube, Gina Guzman asks

00:12:11.357 --> 00:12:12.297
"A question for Laura,

00:12:12.297 --> 00:12:14.337
"according to different home realities,

00:12:14.337 --> 00:12:16.087
"what is the most suitable schedule

00:12:16.087 --> 00:12:17.530
"for distance learning?"

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:19.470
So yeah, what advice would you have?

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.360
- Oh, I mean, I just think

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.820
that we have all been
thrown into a situation

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:25.340
we were not anticipating.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.540
And I feel particularly for

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.640
kids in grades K through two,

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:33.390
where it is very hard for them

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:36.120
first to focus for very
long on a computer.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.260
Secondly, even to use the computer

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.170
to touch the mouse to click on
the wrong window by accident,

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:42.710
their teacher disappears.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.720
We find when we practice our games

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.210
with our staffs, kindergartners,
it's very different.

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:51.210
The parents end up being very involved

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.950
and that is really a strain on a family

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.438
where the parents are working.

00:12:54.438 --> 00:12:56.040
It's also a strain.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.416
I know there's a lot of talk about

00:12:58.416 --> 00:13:00.290
clearly the inequity where

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:03.790
kids don't have a device at home or Wi-Fi,

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:05.490
you have to add in then all the people

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:07.060
where there is a device in the home,

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:09.470
but everybody needs it,
there're multiple kids,

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:11.680
or there's a parent working from home.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:15.310
So I think as far as
what works for schedule,

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:17.890
I think you have every scenario out there.

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.700
As a start, though, it
seems to me the kids

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.450
hit the ground running in the morning,

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:26.950
it is much harder to engage
after lunch during food coma.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:30.590
And I think also that it's really

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:32.850
emotionally healthy for kids to see

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:35.520
their guide, their teacher, if possible,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.603
in the morning at the start of
the day, even if it's brief.

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.900
And then from there, get
their structure for the day

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.030
and move from there.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:46.220
I don't know if that fully
answers the question.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:49.010
But I also think we don't even
really know all the answers

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:51.060
because there's just so
many scenarios out there

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:52.970
and it's really challenging.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:54.700
- Yeah, I think that's
consistency we're seeing.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.360
I mean, what we're
telling very consistent,

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.270
we're saying, hey, just gets started

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:00.670
even if you're able to do
20 minutes, 30 minutes a day

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:03.460
of math reading, maybe writing.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:05.430
That's a start and students won't atrophy.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:07.400
And that I think lowers the stress level.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:08.700
And then you can layer on more.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.450
We've, you know, we've published schedules

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:11.380
and things like that as well.

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:12.570
But I think you're exactly right.

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:13.870
It depends on your circumstance.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.060
And this is a time where people

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:17.530
definitely should not put
more stress on themselves

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:18.460
if they can avoid it.

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:21.790
So no one should beat up on themselves.

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:23.460
- And I was just gonna add one more point

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:25.400
that's kind of related, which is,

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:27.290
I think the fact that we're not,

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:28.940
kids are not with their teachers

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:31.473
is making us more aware
of how powerful that is.

00:14:31.473 --> 00:14:34.430
Tools like Khan Academy and
all the resources out there

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.610
that enable kids to personalize their work

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.210
are incredibly important.

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.740
But we still need to pair a human with it,

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.690
like teachers should never worry

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.550
that any of this is gonna make us

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.150
not have teachers in the future.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:48.180
I think teachers are incredibly important

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.860
and a piece of research,
I was so struck by

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:54.440
is work out of the University
of Washington I-LABS,

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.740
where they do MRIs of babies

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:58.560
they've put, they've created a helmet

00:14:58.560 --> 00:14:59.990
that you put on the baby's head.

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:02.040
And they use math to do motion correction.

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:04.300
And you can watch the baby's
brain while things happen.

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:06.834
And it turns out if you
touch a baby's hand,

00:15:06.834 --> 00:15:09.962
or the baby's mom's hand,
while mom is sitting there,

00:15:09.962 --> 00:15:14.140
Certain parts of the brain
light up, if you show the baby,

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:17.233
a video of her mom, with
her hand being touched,

00:15:17.233 --> 00:15:19.330
those parts don't light up.

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:21.610
We really do react differently,

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:23.180
and in a much more powerful way

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:25.790
when we're with people physically.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:28.080
And so I mean, I'm looking
forward to the end of this

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:29.570
and everybody getting back to school

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:30.930
because I think that

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.894
that's such an important
part of education.

00:15:32.894 --> 00:15:35.390
I'm also hoping though, that we find a way

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:39.220
to pair that with the liberation
I was talking about earlier

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:42.450
of having kids really see what
they need to be working on

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.523
and what they need to master.

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:46.203
- That's fascinating.

00:15:46.203 --> 00:15:48.540
I think it's a super
worth triply underlining,

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:49.800
I think all of us.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:52.570
We always knew the power of
teachers and the work they did,

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:54.570
but I think this is
all driven our empathy,

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:57.250
much, much higher for the amazing work

00:15:57.250 --> 00:16:00.240
they do with our children and sometimes

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.680
how hard it can be.

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:04.080
And I actually wanna make
sure I understand that

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:04.913
that was fascinating.

00:16:04.913 --> 00:16:06.560
You said, obviously, you would expect

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:09.210
that if the child's own hand gets touched

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.900
that would light up parts of their brain.

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:13.390
But you're saying if their
parent is in their presence

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:14.800
or their mother's in their presence,

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:17.280
and someone touched the mother's hand,

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.964
that would light up the brain.

00:16:19.964 --> 00:16:22.270
- Yes, it's mind blowing, because it shows

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:25.930
that infants even a few hours
after birth, have empathy.

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:28.310
I mean, they don't know what a hand is.

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:30.793
They don't know that that's their hand.

00:16:30.793 --> 00:16:33.290
And the mom, like they don't know that

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:35.230
that hand matches their hand

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:36.450
that it's the corresponding point.

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:39.120
And yet, when you touch the
mom's hand, it lights up.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:40.880
I think it's just incredible research.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.361
And they're continuing to do more on this

00:16:43.361 --> 00:16:45.810
out of I-LABS at University of Washington,

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:48.440
but I think it just has
so many implications

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:52.540
for how you want a
blended learning package

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:53.810
where you have the content

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:55.260
where a kid can really work on their own,

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:58.007
but you definitely want a
human in the picture too.

00:16:58.007 --> 00:17:00.520
And we want the best of worlds.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:02.760
- Absolutely and we have
a couple of questions,

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:03.900
which I think is interesting,

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:05.710
especially considering
your background, Laura.

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:09.900
From um, we have a question from Nekeil.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:11.167
Actually, again from YouTube,

00:17:11.167 --> 00:17:15.142
"Sal and Laura, what made you
interested in mathematics?"

00:17:15.142 --> 00:17:16.297
And then for Aditya is asking,

00:17:16.297 --> 00:17:17.890
"How do you make children
interested in math?"

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:19.770
And I'd love to connect
that to your background

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:23.048
you are, if I remember an
astrophysicist by training.

00:17:23.048 --> 00:17:24.135
- I am.

00:17:24.135 --> 00:17:27.380
- So what got you interested in math?

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:29.570
- So, you know, I look back at this

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.870
and I think the thing is
that when I was a kid,

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:36.500
I had a mom and a dad who loved math

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:38.170
and just worked it into the day.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:40.130
Math didn't feel like a separate subject

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:43.852
like I never got flashcards
or worksheets at home.

00:17:43.852 --> 00:17:46.320
My parents both had hobbies

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:49.100
and really would do those with me.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.790
So my mom is an amazing cook.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:53.870
And she had me very early
on measuring with teaspoons

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:55.300
and tablespoons and fractions

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:57.920
and you just get really
fluent in fractions

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:00.450
very, you know a lot earlier
than you do in school.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.860
And then my dad does a lot of carpentry.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:05.980
And he had me using unsafe power tools

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:07.210
at a very young age.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:09.220
And you know what, if you don't
wanna take off your thumb,

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:11.493
you better know what a 16th of an inch is.

00:18:12.467 --> 00:18:15.640
And you're really, you know,
using fractions there too,

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:20.620
as well as tying it with very
physical, tangible objects.

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:22.880
And I think that's part
of it too is for me,

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:24.320
math was always tangible.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:27.360
I'm a very visual and kinetic learner

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:28.740
as opposed to auditory.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:32.160
And that really has led us to
do what we do at Bedtime Math,

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:35.158
where now we have these hands on games.

00:18:35.158 --> 00:18:38.890
And I see how kids who
don't necessarily learn

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:42.530
from hearing a lecture, move
things around on the table

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:44.370
and see it in a completely different way.

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:46.140
And that's what Fun Factor is.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.690
So we're now every Monday and Wednesday

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:50.590
running a live a live webinar

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:52.819
where teachers can get on and find out

00:18:52.819 --> 00:18:55.620
how to do these games with their students.

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:57.040
But families can get on too

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:58.710
I mean, it's just open for anybody

00:18:58.710 --> 00:18:59.980
who'd like to see.

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:02.750
But it's really to bring
the hands on object

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:06.520
back in to pair with the
other forms of learning

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:08.820
'cause kids have different
styles of learning.

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:10.580
- No, that's fascinating.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:12.400
It sounds like a fun household to grow.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:13.520
I agree, I was actually doing

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:14.980
some baking with my kids recently.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:18.090
And, you know, as soon
as you go off the recipe,

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:18.937
and it's like,

00:19:18.937 --> 00:19:19.932
'We're gonna make a two
thirds portion of this,"

00:19:19.932 --> 00:19:24.420
the math, the fraction math
becomes very, very real.

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:25.960
And like, we like to do half the sugar

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:28.090
and it definitely starts hitting you.

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:30.220
And I'd love to follow
up a little bit more

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:31.053
on your background.

00:19:31.053 --> 00:19:34.270
I mean, what what got you into
astrophysics in particular?

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:35.930
I'm guessing from that period

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:38.951
where you were doing fractions
with your mom and dad

00:19:38.951 --> 00:19:41.390
to going into astrophysics.

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:44.400
There's a lot more Math, I
think you must've gotten excited

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:47.560
explain that, what got you interested?

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:50.120
It is a topic I love as well, but...

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:53.470
- Yeah, well actually, I
would say for quite a while

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.310
through elementary and middle school.

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:57.200
I really thought of myself as a math kid.

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:58.050
I loved math.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:00.460
I liked playing with numbers on the side.

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.170
Looking at patterns and perfect squares,

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:05.410
and I was really into numbers
in a very abstract way.

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:08.540
But then I took physics junior year,

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:09.840
and just fell in love with it

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:13.150
'cause my teacher, Dr.
Stoneback was hysterical.

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:15.895
And also just really willing to off road

00:20:15.895 --> 00:20:17.670
willing to take our questions

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.200
and just throw out the lesson plan

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.550
and experiment with whatever
we were asking about.

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:24.400
And I just loved the class.

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:28.360
And then that summer I got
to go to Governor School,

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.320
which is a program that's in many states

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:33.900
and I went for the sciences
and took an astronomy class

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:35.500
and was just blown away by it

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:36.940
and, and fell in love with it.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.460
And I'd always loved the
night sky and been curious,

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.140
but it kind of all came together

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:43.700
when I learned more
about how it all works.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:45.660
So that was...

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:48.500
And I should note that actually...

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:50.980
So what's interesting
is when I was a junior,

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:53.119
that was the year The Challenger blew up,

00:20:53.119 --> 00:20:56.150
and that was a very emotional
thing to see happen,

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:57.968
but it really drove home for me.

00:20:57.968 --> 00:21:00.160
What an amazing thing
these people were going

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:01.240
to go try to do,

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:02.927
I've always just been fascinated by that.

00:21:02.927 --> 00:21:05.270
And for a while I wanted
to be an astronaut,

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:08.840
but I have lazy eye,
which is not correctable.

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:10.570
So I can't be a pilot.

00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:14.650
And at the time very few people
were Mission Specialists.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:19.040
So to go on and get the PhD
and hope to be an astronaut,

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:22.780
it just seemed like not
a terribly likely path.

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:24.890
So sadly, I did not pursue it.

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:26.280
But I am so excited

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:29.330
that we are hopefully gonna
have recreational space travel

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:31.410
for all of us in the next few years.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:33.230
That's what I'm hoping for.

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:35.008
- Yeah, no space is
definitely one of the topics

00:21:35.008 --> 00:21:37.240
that I'm fascinated
and my astronaut dreams

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:38.450
I squashed them myself.

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:40.790
Once I realized how claustrophobic I am.

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:44.480
I do not have the right
stuff, so to speak.

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:46.163
But I wanna discover.

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:49.106
I wanna know what we discover

00:21:49.106 --> 00:21:51.180
as we learn more about the universe

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:53.040
and travel to other places

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:54.230
and I'll just throw out you know,

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:57.190
my own quick response for that math.

00:21:57.190 --> 00:21:58.970
You know, when you really
just peel the envelope

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:01.950
of understanding reality,
math is the purest way

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:03.040
of understanding reality.

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:06.190
Everything else has a layer of our senses

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:08.370
and our mental models that we construct.

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:09.550
But math is so pure.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:10.730
And you know, I've said this other times,

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:14.010
if we ever have to communicate
with another alien life form,

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:15.850
you know, staying on the space theme,

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:17.520
the way that we could
actually probably communicate

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:19.100
that each of us are intelligent

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:20.636
is probably through math,

00:22:20.636 --> 00:22:21.469
I don't know if we'll be able to

00:22:21.469 --> 00:22:22.760
communicate well beyond that,

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:23.593
but we can at least say,

00:22:23.593 --> 00:22:24.426
"Look, no, we're thinking,

00:22:24.426 --> 00:22:25.937
"look, here's some prime numbers."

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:29.960
- Yeah and I should note,
also, just one point from that

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:32.070
was that I hit a point

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:33.970
where math connected with something

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:35.120
that I fell in love with.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:38.150
And I think when we talk about
what helps kids love math,

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:40.440
seeing the real life application,

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:44.410
seeing exciting jobs
that use math, you know,

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:46.400
kids are so into video games,

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:48.840
all that programming behind the scenes,

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:50.470
there's a ton of math in there

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:54.160
and, you know, just connecting
the dots like that for kids,

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:55.880
I think is really important.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:57.630
I was lucky to have that.

00:22:57.630 --> 00:22:58.463
- One interesting question.

00:22:58.463 --> 00:23:00.809
You know, I saw this with
my cousin, Nadia when I,

00:23:00.809 --> 00:23:02.460
you know, this is kind of the famous

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:03.450
how Khan Academy started.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:05.210
She was 12 years old,
she had trouble in math.

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.440
And I remember even when I
started tutoring her, you know,

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:09.733
this was back in 2004.

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:12.327
You know, she had already
convinced herself,

00:23:12.327 --> 00:23:14.733
"I'm not good at math,
math is not my thing."

00:23:14.733 --> 00:23:18.760
I suspect that some of it has
to do with gender stereotypes,

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:21.640
maybe messages she got
from the broader world.

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:23.620
Clearly, that did not happen to you.

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:25.460
Why do you think that does happen?

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:28.080
And how, if you're a
parent, and I do believe,

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:29.750
especially in that middle school age,

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:31.490
that's when people start
to perceive themselves.

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:33.350
I'm a math person or
I'm this type of person

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:34.476
or I'm not that type of person.

00:23:34.476 --> 00:23:36.490
What advice do you have for parents

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:41.430
to kinda, to deprogram that
that social programming?

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:44.410
- Yeah, there is so much it
is such a systemic problem

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:47.730
coming from all angles,
and especially with girls.

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:49.310
I think that, you know,

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:51.420
I think it's simpler than
we sometimes make it.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:54.210
It's really important to taste victory.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:57.240
And to have another person
validate that for you

00:23:57.240 --> 00:23:59.513
and say, "You know, you are really good."

00:23:59.513 --> 00:24:01.708
It's funny because I've
never had math anxiety

00:24:01.708 --> 00:24:05.090
and always loved math, but
my equivalent, I would say,

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:07.180
was gym anxiety when I was a kid

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:09.050
because I had skipped a grade.

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:12.390
So I was young, and I was
also small for my age.

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:14.060
And I did ballet, I was very dainty.

00:24:14.060 --> 00:24:15.770
I was not remotely athletic.

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:19.030
And in gym when they'd go
to pick teams over and over,

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:20.392
I was always picked last,

00:24:20.392 --> 00:24:22.230
Then I would do badly because of course,

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:24.660
I wasn't confident, that
would validate for everybody.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:26.140
Yep, you wanna pick her last.

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:27.250
And this went on for years.

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:28.760
And it was terribly humiliating.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:30.390
I mean, this was painful.

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:34.450
And then in ninth grade, we
did presidential fitness.

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:37.570
And one of the events is the high jump

00:24:37.570 --> 00:24:40.040
and thanks to my springiness from ballet,

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:42.290
I beat the whole grade in the high jump

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:43.460
never having done it.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:46.739
And the gym teacher who till
then thought it was a loser

00:24:46.739 --> 00:24:48.317
said to me, "Oh my gosh,

00:24:48.317 --> 00:24:51.210
"we need you on the track and
field team get out there."

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:52.680
And that was just life changing.

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:54.624
I went and I ended up being a hurdler.

00:24:54.624 --> 00:24:58.690
I never would have done that
if I hadn't had a moment

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:01.310
where I actually succeeded at something,

00:25:01.310 --> 00:25:04.927
and to have the adults say,
"No, you're not imagining that

00:25:04.927 --> 00:25:05.940
"you really did it."

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:07.230
So I thought a lot about

00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:08.143
how do we bring this to math?

00:25:08.143 --> 00:25:11.480
Because I think math anxiety
is so similar, you know,

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:13.980
Sian Beilock, who's done amazing studies

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:16.330
on the neuroscience behind all this

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:19.021
has shown that when, if
you're afraid of math,

00:25:19.021 --> 00:25:20.810
when you do math problems,

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:23.700
that fear actually blocks
your working memory,

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:26.409
and it becomes a self fulfilling prophecy.

00:25:26.409 --> 00:25:29.210
So, you know, it's really important

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:30.770
to feel confident with math.

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:32.962
But this also gets back to giving kids

00:25:32.962 --> 00:25:35.270
the right challenge to be working on

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:36.800
so they do master it.

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:39.270
And that's why you don't
want those Swiss cheese holes

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:40.330
in the learning, right?

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:42.976
You wanna figure out, what
do kids need to fill in,

00:25:42.976 --> 00:25:44.600
have them work at it.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:46.570
And it might mean going
back to kindergarten

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:48.749
and starting with the really basic stuff.

00:25:48.749 --> 00:25:50.900
This is what we do with our math problem.

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:53.473
Every little fun story
about ninjas or flamingos

00:25:53.473 --> 00:25:56.310
kicks off with a Pre-K question.

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:58.977
And I think having kids go
to the beginning do that.

00:25:58.977 --> 00:26:01.160
And it's like "Yeah, you got that see?"

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:02.817
Okay, let's do the little kid question.

00:26:02.817 --> 00:26:05.570
"You know how to count to
five, see, you've got this,"

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:08.320
and working on up, kids
need to taste victory.

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:10.243
And I think that really
it is as simple as that,

00:26:10.243 --> 00:26:12.340
that that turns it around.

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:14.250
- I love that because, you know,

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:16.371
when you're going through
your stories of your life

00:26:16.371 --> 00:26:18.640
and me reflect a little bit on my stories,

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:20.235
and I think you're right, you know,

00:26:20.235 --> 00:26:23.010
early on, I had probably a little bit

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:24.610
of communication anxiety.

00:26:24.610 --> 00:26:27.180
I was actually in speech
therapy when I was very young.

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:29.780
And I didn't, and I was
very shy, and actually,

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:32.563
I'm still more introverted
than most people suspect.

00:26:34.990 --> 00:26:36.093
I'm good at acting.

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:39.610
I like being with people.

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:41.570
Actually, I really like
conversations like this.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:43.900
I don't like cocktail
parties where you know,

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:45.780
you have to make small talk
and then move on to the next

00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:47.990
and you kind of have to
move into someone else.

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:50.650
That's not, that I'd rather
just hide in the bathroom.

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:53.030
But the but you're right.

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:54.314
I mean, those times that I got

00:26:54.314 --> 00:26:57.353
my aperture open about times about myself,

00:26:57.353 --> 00:26:58.740
maybe I can communicate,

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:00.350
maybe I could be a good writer,

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:01.700
was when I did something

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:03.070
and someone gave that positive feedback.

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:04.170
And I knew it was authentic.

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:06.439
It wasn't just, they were just
trying to make me feel good.

00:27:06.439 --> 00:27:09.013
But that did feed on itself.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:12.110
- It does and then it becomes true.

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:12.943
It really does.

00:27:12.943 --> 00:27:13.950
- Something there.

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:16.300
And so, when we have
these good conversations

00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:17.530
time goes by super fast.

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:19.860
But I think we have time
for a few more questions.

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:22.507
So from YouTube Esi Sanchez,

00:27:22.507 --> 00:27:23.857
"A question for Miss Overdeck.

00:27:23.857 --> 00:27:26.567
"What is it like knowing
something from a kid's bedroom

00:27:26.567 --> 00:27:27.830
"became so big."

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:29.320
You know, so they're
talking about Bedtime Math.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:31.280
And I guess I'll add to that question,

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:33.270
what advice do you have
for folks, you know,

00:27:33.270 --> 00:27:35.460
both your narrative and my narrative,

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.030
Khan Academy and Bedtime Math,

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:39.670
these all started from family
projects, very organic,

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:42.070
but something happened
in your mind, in my mind,

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:44.320
so like maybe even more people
could benefit from this.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:45.960
So what advice would
you have for other folks

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:48.910
to kind of solve big problems like that?

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:53.028
- Yeah, so as far as
how it feels, you know,

00:27:53.028 --> 00:27:56.507
it really took off very fast.

00:27:56.507 --> 00:28:00.130
I started sharing math problems
with families in our town.

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:01.700
And the list grew really fast,

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:04.040
people started telling
other people about it.

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:05.660
But I got hit by lightning,

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:07.230
The New York Times reviewed my blog

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:09.170
about a month after I started it.

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:10.280
That just isn't fair, right?

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:13.200
And so that brought in
thousands of people.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:14.980
And it then grew very fast.

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:16.770
On the other hand, I am a math person,

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:20.430
I'm always looking at the zeros
on the ends of the numbers.

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:23.270
And I know that there are 25
million kids in our country

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:26.160
in Bedtime Math's target market,

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:27.670
and we don't have anywhere near

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:29.470
we do not have enough of those zeros.

00:28:29.470 --> 00:28:32.800
So it's big, and yet it's small,

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:34.628
it's really hard to move the needle.

00:28:34.628 --> 00:28:38.480
I guess we've had over a
quarter million downloads

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:41.200
of our app, but that's 1% of the market.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:44.440
So I wake, that gets me
out of bed every morning

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:46.653
thinking about how to
get it to more people.

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:50.020
And as far as like, what clicked

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:53.610
and, you know, again, it
was actually external people

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:55.287
because it was other people saying,

00:28:55.287 --> 00:28:56.830
"Hey, what are you doing?"

00:28:56.830 --> 00:28:58.957
I mean, it's not too different
from the gym teacher saying,

00:28:58.957 --> 00:29:00.177
"Hey, wait a minute.

00:29:00.177 --> 00:29:02.229
"Look what you're doing,
go do that bigger."

00:29:02.229 --> 00:29:06.530
You know, I think that the
more we encourage each other

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:08.530
and unfortunately, you know,

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:10.440
we don't always have
opportunities to do this

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:11.780
or don't do it as much as we should.

00:29:11.780 --> 00:29:13.580
But when we encourage others,

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:16.530
I think we don't even
realize the massive impact

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:19.360
we might be having at that
moment without realizing it

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:23.203
'cause it can actually steer
people's trajectory completely.

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:26.870
- And one interesting you just
made a small comment, like,

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:27.780
you know, "I got struck by lightning,"

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:29.560
implying that you know, you
got lucky in New York Times,

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:30.720
but I think there's something there,

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:33.360
which is, in order to get
struck by that lightning,

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:34.950
you have to build a bit of a lightning rod

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:35.820
and obviously, literally,

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:37.210
I don't want any of the lightning rods

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:40.356
to get struck by lightning
but figuratively,

00:29:40.356 --> 00:29:42.813
by putting that out there.

00:29:44.330 --> 00:29:46.560
Yes, you wouldn't want to...

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:48.310
Invent it incidentally,
by Benjamin Franklin.

00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:49.904
He doesn't get enough credit,
I think as a scientist,

00:29:49.904 --> 00:29:54.904
but anyway, but you don't.

00:29:55.167 --> 00:29:57.280
A lot of things, you can't
put in a strategic plan

00:29:57.280 --> 00:29:59.460
that the New York Times will
write about us on this date,

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:01.687
and same thing similar things
happen in Khan Academy.

00:30:01.687 --> 00:30:04.390
But the fact that you took
it as a family project,

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:07.010
you allowed that feedback,

00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:08.810
you started scaling it up.

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:11.280
And then it was there to
be discovered, I think,

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:14.130
is a interesting layer there.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:17.070
- Yeah and to that point,
'cause I totally agree with you,

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:18.570
you'd have to just take your thing

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:21.020
and get out there with
it and see what happens.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:22.580
And this reminds me of another point,

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:24.850
which is that you are not
looking for perfection,

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:28.240
when you do that, waiting
for your thing to be perfect.

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:29.910
You will never get it out there.

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:31.870
There's a great book called Lean Startup.

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:33.110
It's one of my favorite books

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:35.011
and they talk about
minimum viable product.

00:30:35.011 --> 00:30:37.749
You take basic idea,

00:30:37.749 --> 00:30:39.650
don't put a whole lot of effort to it,

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:41.210
just come up with what it is.

00:30:41.210 --> 00:30:43.447
Put it out there and let
the market say to you,

00:30:43.447 --> 00:30:44.647
"You know, we love that.

00:30:44.647 --> 00:30:45.957
"But could you make it purple?

00:30:45.957 --> 00:30:47.567
"Or could you make it twice the size

00:30:47.567 --> 00:30:49.040
"or could you deliver it faster."

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:53.630
Or whatever it is, and all
our growth at Bedtime Math

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:56.410
a lot of it has been from
people giving us that feedback.

00:30:56.410 --> 00:30:57.530
And of course for you,

00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:00.820
I know you were doing your
videos for your cousin.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:02.070
And I'm sure you were just kind of

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:03.290
whipping those together for her

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:04.700
and then look at what has happened.

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:06.230
I mean, it's the exact same thing.

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:08.150
If you'd waited for them to be perfect,

00:31:08.150 --> 00:31:10.030
and frankly, they were
close to perfect already,

00:31:10.030 --> 00:31:11.729
but if you wait for them to be perfect,

00:31:11.729 --> 00:31:13.580
it might have never happened.

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:15.110
And I think a lot of times

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:17.920
people feel a little bit of paralysis

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:19.760
and it's better to just take the risk

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:24.760
and take the thing 80%, 90%
done and just get out there.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:25.703
- Yeah and I'll add to that,

00:31:25.703 --> 00:31:27.280
I think there might be
an element that sometimes

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:29.630
what you think is perfect is less perfect

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:32.390
than the thing that's more
authentic and a little raw.

00:31:32.390 --> 00:31:33.810
I mean, you know, that's
why I kind of nodded my head

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:35.630
when you said, you know,
the Khan Academy videos,

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:36.800
they still have a little bit of that,

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:38.680
you know, I still make it
here at this desk right here

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:39.910
with this microphone.

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:41.360
And there's still these hand drawn things.

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:42.865
We've improved it a reasonable bit,

00:31:42.865 --> 00:31:45.540
but I think if someone
had given me you know,

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:47.250
a large grant from the government

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:50.060
or if I was working for a
large publisher 15 years ago

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:51.150
and said make math videos,

00:31:51.150 --> 00:31:52.320
I would have fallen into the trap

00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:53.153
that a lot of people have

00:31:53.153 --> 00:31:55.340
with making these fairly
well produced things,

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:56.707
but they read like, you know,

00:31:56.707 --> 00:31:59.410
"Welcome to the next step in
quad factoring quadratics,"

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:00.750
it feels like it GPS device

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:01.770
and people don't connect with it

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:03.860
and there's an authenticity around

00:32:05.210 --> 00:32:09.570
sometimes the 80% solution
so, 100% agree with you.

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:12.230
So maybe finish on one question

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:13.920
'cause you just talked
about this paralysis

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:15.455
of sometimes when people wanna do anything

00:32:15.455 --> 00:32:19.040
they want the perfect becomes
the enemy of the good.

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:21.578
Niashu on YouTube asks,

00:32:21.578 --> 00:32:24.417
"What's the trick for not getting afraid

00:32:24.417 --> 00:32:26.217
"of the word math?

00:32:26.217 --> 00:32:28.387
"I mean how to overcome the problem,

00:32:28.387 --> 00:32:30.581
"especially when preparing
for some entrance exam,"

00:32:30.581 --> 00:32:33.627
like for I guess in their
situation, the GMAT.

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:36.473
What advice I mean,

00:32:36.473 --> 00:32:38.290
you're someone who's
never had math anxiety.

00:32:38.290 --> 00:32:39.340
Do you empathize with that

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:40.530
and what advice do you have

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:42.500
and I'm happy to give
my two cents as well.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:44.500
- You know, I do empathize

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:47.670
because I mean math is a four
letter word unfortunately

00:32:47.670 --> 00:32:48.850
for a lot of people

00:32:48.850 --> 00:32:52.010
and I've seen this and
I've had fans email in

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:54.240
and just talk about this with their kids.

00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:55.340
So I think there are a couple things

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:58.800
one is expanding what we think math is

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:03.103
For instance there's a lot
of spatial stuff in math,

00:33:03.103 --> 00:33:06.274
spatial relations, rotation of objects,

00:33:06.274 --> 00:33:08.290
that's all math too.

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:10.860
And a lot of people, that's a
different part of the brain.

00:33:10.860 --> 00:33:12.610
And a lot of people are good at that,

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:14.640
and don't ever really get to show it off.

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:17.079
In fact, you can see behind
me these little tiles,

00:33:17.079 --> 00:33:18.500
this is one of our games

00:33:18.500 --> 00:33:21.680
that we're doing on our
webinars, these Fun Factor games,

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:23.620
where kids make these squares

00:33:23.620 --> 00:33:25.430
and have to match them to patterns.

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:26.840
And sometimes you find that kids

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:29.203
who are not particularly
good at math in school,

00:33:29.203 --> 00:33:31.500
are just rock stars at this

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:34.228
and saying, you know what, that's math.

00:33:34.228 --> 00:33:35.260
You're good at that.

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:37.420
Again, you have to taste of victory.

00:33:37.420 --> 00:33:39.133
And then I think also, as far as

00:33:39.133 --> 00:33:41.550
how do we make math not a bad word?

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:43.630
We are all in this together.

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:44.877
I hear people say, "You know,

00:33:44.877 --> 00:33:45.830
"I'm just not good at math."

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:47.496
Or, "I hate math."

00:33:47.496 --> 00:33:51.220
A study showed that
something like 72% of moms

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:52.970
think they're not qualified

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:55.450
to help their kids with
their math homework.

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:56.720
That is a thing.

00:33:56.720 --> 00:34:00.030
That's a deep conversation
we have to have as a country.

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:01.329
To kind of get over that, because really,

00:34:01.329 --> 00:34:03.590
I really think anyone can do math.

00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:05.524
And what you want is for the parent,

00:34:05.524 --> 00:34:07.940
to learn the math with
the child, the parent,

00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:10.860
the guardian, whoever it
is, and get comfortable.

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:13.437
And even if adults are not
comfortable with something,

00:34:13.437 --> 00:34:16.400
we shouldn't say it in front of the kids.

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:17.720
Like just keep it to yourself

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:21.240
because kids don't start
off not liking math.

00:34:21.240 --> 00:34:22.073
They love it.

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:25.800
They come to our Crazy
Eights after school club,

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:27.500
and they have a great time

00:34:27.500 --> 00:34:29.750
and they actually don't even believe

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:32.145
it's the same thing they're
doing in school, it is math.

00:34:32.145 --> 00:34:35.099
They don't start off not liking it,

00:34:35.099 --> 00:34:38.360
we're causing that, so
we can solve the problem.

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:39.193
It's up to us.

00:34:39.193 --> 00:34:41.688
- Yeah, 100% agree because
math at the end of the day,

00:34:41.688 --> 00:34:43.780
it's puzzles, it's logic

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:47.690
and all of us are drawn
to games, puzzles, logic,

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:50.410
and that's in the math
even in the academic math,

00:34:50.410 --> 00:34:52.060
but sometimes that gets lost

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:54.676
and so my only two cents to
add to what Laura added is

00:34:54.676 --> 00:34:56.380
you know whether math is your phobia,

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:58.610
public speaking is your
phobia writing is or phobia,

00:34:58.610 --> 00:35:01.010
whatever it is gym class is your phobia.

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:02.324
I think it's the paralysis of

00:35:02.324 --> 00:35:05.180
sometimes wanting the perfect
to be is the enemy of good

00:35:05.180 --> 00:35:07.690
Sometimes it's, you feel like
the world will end you know,

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:10.200
my son was getting very
nervous for a piano recital

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:12.197
and he's like, and I'm
like, "Iran it's not like

00:35:12.197 --> 00:35:13.310
"it's gonna be the end of the world."

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:15.162
And his response is "What if it is,"

00:35:15.162 --> 00:35:18.330
you know, like what if the
earth parts and lava erupts

00:35:18.330 --> 00:35:21.560
because he makes a blunder
on Taco Bell's Cannon.

00:35:21.560 --> 00:35:22.640
I mean, it was a fun moment,

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:24.510
but we just have to remember the Earth

00:35:24.510 --> 00:35:26.780
is not gonna end you just
give your best attempt.

00:35:26.780 --> 00:35:28.770
And at the end of the day,

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:31.200
you are not your math scores or,

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:33.270
or whatever else and that
will actually liberate you

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.130
to do better and to Laura's point

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:36.280
for all these people with math anxiety,

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:37.130
just get started.

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:38.657
Just do, you know, to quote Frozen Two,

00:35:38.657 --> 00:35:40.400
"Do the next right thing."

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:41.640
And for all those parents out there,

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:43.850
if you put in even 10, 15
minutes on Khan Academy,

00:35:43.850 --> 00:35:46.260
you can learn it for
your kids and model it.

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:48.567
So Laura, you know, one last question.

00:35:48.567 --> 00:35:51.720
I want to be conscientious
of your of your time.

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:53.650
You know, what messages do you have

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:54.769
for all everyone out there

00:35:54.769 --> 00:35:56.770
who's socially distanced

00:35:56.770 --> 00:35:59.313
families are trying to
keep the learning going.

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:03.440
Well, how can they make, what
are the silver linings here?

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:06.250
What are some ideas that you
you're able to experience

00:36:06.250 --> 00:36:07.970
in your own family that maybe other people

00:36:07.970 --> 00:36:10.143
can do to cope with what's going on?

00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:14.240
- Well, I do think that one silver lining

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:17.230
is that families are kind
of now learning together

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:20.270
in a way that they don't when
the kids are off at school,

00:36:20.270 --> 00:36:22.613
and everybody else is home or at work.

00:36:23.490 --> 00:36:27.040
So I think, loosening up
what we think learning is

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:29.390
and what we think education is.

00:36:29.390 --> 00:36:31.610
There's some funny
videos out there saying,

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:33.470
you know, "Household chores can be math,"

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:34.570
or, "Cooking could be math,"

00:36:34.570 --> 00:36:37.120
But it's actually, I mean,
maybe it's not the chores.

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:40.030
But there are a lot of things
that just go on in your house

00:36:40.030 --> 00:36:41.390
that involve numbers.

00:36:41.390 --> 00:36:44.030
There are a lot of fun
things that involve reading.

00:36:44.030 --> 00:36:47.700
And I think loosening up
what we think learning is

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:49.110
I think we get in a track

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:51.067
where we see what goes
on in school and we say,

00:36:51.067 --> 00:36:52.470
"Oh, that's what learning is."

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:54.020
And then you leave school and come home

00:36:54.020 --> 00:36:56.370
and other stuff happens.

00:36:56.370 --> 00:36:57.720
We are learning all the time

00:36:57.720 --> 00:36:59.040
and we're just now more aware

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:00.780
that you can do a lot of learning at home

00:37:00.780 --> 00:37:02.204
because when you're home all day,

00:37:02.204 --> 00:37:05.590
you suddenly have all
this time to explore,

00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:07.300
it is really hard being home,

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:10.260
it's really hard not being
able to get supplies,

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:12.830
food, everything is harder right now.

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:14.830
And to varying degrees for people.

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:18.137
But I think just knowing that this is,

00:37:18.137 --> 00:37:20.970
it's temporary, and
let's make the most of it

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:24.080
and really get to bond over
other kinds of learning.

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:26.410
And I mean, it really
is the time to pull out

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:30.170
the board games, the decks of
cards, the dice, poker chips,

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:33.060
everything, 'cause all
of that is learning too

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:35.773
and let's take advantage of
the fun time and do that.

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:37.750
- That's great advice.

00:37:37.750 --> 00:37:39.330
Laura, thanks so much for joining

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:40.666
and answering our questions.

00:37:40.666 --> 00:37:42.750
- Oh, and thank you so much for having me

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:43.890
and for doing this show

00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:45.610
'cause I know you at Khan Academy

00:37:45.610 --> 00:37:48.180
have had an explosion of
traffic this last month

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:50.590
and have your hands full so it's awesome

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:51.638
you're doing this show and--

00:37:51.638 --> 00:37:54.523
- Oh no, this has been a
blast for us too, thank you.

00:37:55.440 --> 00:37:58.460
Well, everyone another, you know,

00:37:58.460 --> 00:37:59.973
always fun to talk to Laura.

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:02.280
Thanks for joining this.

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:04.810
I think we got some good
advice today and you know,

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:07.150
stay safe, stay healthy as we go through

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:08.170
these unusual times.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:09.620
But there are some silver linings

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:11.100
some ways to keep learning

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:13.260
some ways to get even
more connected with family

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:14.840
and discover things about the universe

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:16.200
that you might not have had the time

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:17.420
or the space to do in the past

00:38:17.420 --> 00:38:20.340
and we hope to help you
or help you help others,

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:23.250
if you're a parent or
teacher in any way we can.

00:38:23.250 --> 00:38:25.990
So we look forward to
what is today Thursday?

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:28.203
We look forward to seeing you tomorrow.

00:38:29.070 --> 00:38:31.392
Have a good night or evening,
afternoon, I don't know.

00:38:31.392 --> 00:38:34.309
(giggling lightly)

